<section id="methodology" class="mt-16 pt-10 border-t border-neutral-200">
  <div class="max-w-3xl">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading text-neutral-900 mb-3">
      How AP® U.S. History Scores Are Calculated: The Official Formula
    </h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      The College Board converts your raw performance across all four sections of the AP® U.S. History exam into a standardized composite score on a scale of 0 to 100. Rather than simply averaging your percentages, each section is weighted according to strict psychometric guidelines.
    </p>

    <!-- Formula Table -->
    <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-neutral-200/90 bg-white shadow-xs mb-8">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-xs border-collapse">
        <thead class="bg-neutral-50 text-neutral-600 font-semibold border-b border-neutral-200 uppercase tracking-wider text-[11px]">
          <tr>
            <th class="p-3.5">Exam Section</th>
            <th class="p-3.5 text-center">Raw Max</th>
            <th class="p-3.5 text-center">Exam Weight</th>
            <th class="p-3.5 text-right">Composite Formula</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-neutral-100 text-neutral-700">
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3.5 font-bold text-neutral-900">Section I, Part A: Multiple Choice (MCQ)</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 text-center font-mono">55</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 text-center font-semibold text-blue-700">40%</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 text-right font-mono text-neutral-600">(MCQ / 55) × 40</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3.5 font-bold text-neutral-900">Section I, Part B: Short-Answer (SAQ)</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 text-center font-mono">9</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 text-center font-semibold text-blue-700">20%</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 text-right font-mono text-neutral-600">(SAQ / 9) × 20</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3.5 font-bold text-neutral-900">Section II, Part A: Document-Based Question (DBQ)</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 text-center font-mono">7</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 text-center font-semibold text-blue-700">25%</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 text-right font-mono text-neutral-600">(DBQ / 7) × 25</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3.5 font-bold text-neutral-900">Section II, Part B: Long Essay Question (LEQ)</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 text-center font-mono">6</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 text-center font-semibold text-blue-700">15%</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 text-right font-mono text-neutral-600">(LEQ / 6) × 15</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
        <tfoot>
          <tr class="bg-neutral-50/70 border-t border-neutral-200 font-bold text-neutral-900">
            <td class="p-3.5">Total Weighted Composite Score</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 text-center font-mono">77 Raw</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 text-center text-blue-700">100%</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 text-right font-mono text-blue-700">Sum of All 4 Sections</td>
          </tr>
        </tfoot>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- Equating & Cutoff Details -->
    <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold font-heading text-neutral-900">
        Understanding the AP Curve and Psychometric Equating
      </h3>
      <p>
        A common misconception is that the APUSH curve is graded on a traditional bell curve that pits students against each other. In reality, the College Board uses a process called <strong>equating</strong>. Equating ensures that student scores reflect an absolute standard of college-level competence, rather than arbitrary quotas.
      </p>
      <p>
        If an operational exam administration contains slightly more challenging primary source stimulus questions or a particularly demanding DBQ prompt, the composite cutoff required for a 4 or 5 may be calibrated down by 1 to 3 composite points. Conversely, a more approachable form will have slightly higher cutoffs.
      </p>
      <p>
        Historically across released administrations (such as 2017, 2020, and recent standardized models), composite scores above <strong>74 to 75</strong> reliably achieve a <strong>5</strong>, composite scores between <strong>60 and 73</strong> achieve a <strong>4</strong>, and scores between <strong>47 and 59</strong> earn a passing score of <strong>3</strong>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
